INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(159,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/local-ai-rtx-spark-launch-event-invite/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/local-ai-rtx-spark-launch-event-invite','Be There for the Beginning: Celebrate the Launch of NVIDIA RTX Spark','bethereforthebeginningcelebratethelaunchofnvidiartxspark','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-15T17:00:23Z','2026-10-17T08:44:21Z','A new beginning for Windows PCs starts tomorrow with the launch of NVIDIA RTX Spark — one PC built for creators, gamers and AI agents. All are invited to celebrate with NVIDIA and partners at launch events around the world. Share Your Spark Every PC has a story. What was your first Windows PC, and […]',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(160,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nemotron-3-5-super-vl/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nemotron-3-5-super-vl','Open From Data Center to Desktop: NVIDIA Expands Nemotron Family With Highest Efficiency Super VL and Bolt Models','openfromdatacentertodesktopnvidiaexpandsnemotronfamilywithhighestefficiencysupervlandboltmodels','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-15T15:00:24Z','2026-10-17T08:44:21Z','NVIDIA today released Nemotron 3.5 Super VL, an open 120-billion-parameter model that delivers the fastest task completion and highest accuracy for specialized always-on agents. Joining it are two new edge models: Nemotron 3.5 Bolt 74B-A9B 24GB, optimized to balance intelligence and speed per GB for local agentic workloads, and Nemotron 3.5 Bolt E4B 4GB, optimized […]',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(161,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/geforce-now-thursday-enshrouded-update/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/geforce-now-thursday-enshrouded-update','Dial Up the Cloud: NVIDIA DLSS 4.5 Controls Arrive on GeForce NOW','dialupthecloudnvidiadlss45controlsarriveongeforcenow','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-15T13:00:59Z','2026-10-17T08:44:21Z','Dial in the next gaming session this GFN Thursday. New NVIDIA DLSS 4.5 controls give GeForce NOW members more choice over how supported games look and perform in the cloud, even without the latest hardware. Then, step into the Shroud as Keen Games’ Enshrouded leaves early access with its 1.0 release, or let the bolts […]',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(162,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/ec-scientific-computing-pledge/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/ec-scientific-computing-pledge','NVIDIA Expands Advanced Technology Access for Europe’s Scientific Community Through Open Learning Resources, Collaborations and Compute','nvidiaexpandsadvancedtechnologyaccessforeuropesscientificcommunitythroughopenlearningresourcescollaborationsandcompute','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-15T10:00:12Z','2026-10-17T08:44:21Z','Much of today’s scientific progress begins with computation. But turning accelerated computing’s potential into scientific progress — modeling climate systems, designing molecules, simulating materials and more — depends on more than breakthrough technology. Especially for smaller labs, early-career scientists or science-based startups, the hardest part of research can be everything that comes before the science: […]',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(163,'https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nordics-technology-ecosystem/','https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nordics-technology-ecosystem','The Nordics and NVIDIA Show What’s Possible When a Region Commits to Advanced Computing','thenordicsandnvidiashowwhatspossiblewhenaregioncommitstoadvancedcomputing','nvidia_blog','NVIDIA blog','en','2026-10-15T05:00:12Z','2026-10-17T08:44:21Z','The Nordic countries have become the European leader in unicorn startup density, producing the most emerging companies valued at $1 billion or more per capita on the continent. Now, a new chapter for the region is unfolding as countries race to build one of Europe’s most consequential technology ecosystems. The many computing advancements already underway […]',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(164,'https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/proof-based-general-purpose-computing-with-magnetar/','https://microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/proof-based-general-purpose-computing-with-magnetar','Proof-based general-purpose computing with Magnetar','proofbasedgeneralpurposecomputingwithmagnetar','msr_blog','Microsoft Research blog','en','2026-10-15T16:00:00Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','Zero-knowledge proofs can verify computing results without rerunning workloads or exposing sensitive data. Magnetar helps bring them into the mainstream with support for standard programming languages, major speed gains, and quantum-resistant security. The post Proof-based general-purpose computing with Magnetar appeared first on Microsoft Research .',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(165,'https://eos.org/research-spotlights/the-ambiguous-costs-of-climate-change','https://eos.org/research-spotlights/the-ambiguous-costs-of-climate-change','The Ambiguous Costs of Climate Change','theambiguouscostsofclimatechange','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-16T13:28:13Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','In 2017, heavy rain from Hurricane Harvey flooded Texas neighborhoods and caused billions of dollars in damages. The aftermath is seen in this satellite image of an area near Houston. Credit: Sentinel Hub/Flickr , CC BY 2.0',3.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(166,'https://eos.org/research-spotlights/clouds-over-the-highest-mountains-dance-a-little-differently','https://eos.org/research-spotlights/clouds-over-the-highest-mountains-dance-a-little-differently','Clouds over the Highest Mountains Dance a Little Differently','cloudsoverthehighestmountainsdancealittledifferently','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-16T13:26:26Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','Researchers studied how clouds form over mountain ranges, such as the Andes, seen here, to learn more about how this formation could affect local weather patterns. Credit: Nicolas Prieto / Unsplash',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(167,'https://eos.org/editor-highlights/melting-permafrost-impacts-arctic-rivers-in-vulnerable-regions','https://eos.org/editor-highlights/melting-permafrost-impacts-arctic-rivers-in-vulnerable-regions','Melting Permafrost Impacts Arctic Rivers in Vulnerable Regions','meltingpermafrostimpactsarcticriversinvulnerableregions','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-15T16:28:25Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','The image shows evidence of acid inputs resulting from the weathering of sulfide minerals, which leach into a stream from a hillslope. The red color is due to the precipitation of iron and aluminum oxides in the stream as added acidity is buffered. Although these processes remove some metals, rivers downstream of such point sources have higher levels of sulfate and metals, including manganese, zinc, nickel, cadmium and rare earths. Credit: Evinger et al. [2026] , Figure S1e',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(168,'https://eos.org/research-spotlights/measuring-deep-ocean-oxygen-with-microscopic-magnetic-fossils','https://eos.org/research-spotlights/measuring-deep-ocean-oxygen-with-microscopic-magnetic-fossils','Measuring Deep-Ocean Oxygen with Microscopic Magnetic Fossils','measuringdeepoceanoxygenwithmicroscopicmagneticfossils','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-15T13:05:38Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','Scientists used clues from magnetotactic bacteria, the organelles of which are seen here, to learn more about ocean temperatures in Earth’s distant past. Credit: Frank Mickoleit/Wikimedia Commons , CC BY-SA 3.0',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(169,'https://eos.org/articles/a-hydrological-paradox-on-the-silk-road','https://eos.org/articles/a-hydrological-paradox-on-the-silk-road','A Hydrological Paradox on the Silk Road','ahydrologicalparadoxonthesilkroad','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-15T13:02:53Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','New research investigates why trade prospered in Central Asia between about 600 and 1100 CE despite arid conditions. This mural, created during the Tang Dynasty in the early 700s, shows the Buddha preaching, as well as travelers on what could be a Silk Road trading post. Credit: Unknown 8th century mural painter/Wikimedia Commons , Public Domain',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(170,'https://eos.org/thelandslideblog/landslides-in-an-el-nino-year','https://eos.org/thelandslideblog/landslides-in-an-el-nino-year','Landslides in an El Niño year','landslidesinanelninoyear','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-15T07:16:19Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','Global map of where El Nino Southern Oscillation (ENSO) most strongly impacts population exposure to landslide hazards. From Emberson et al. (2021), published under a CC License.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(171,'https://eos.org/research-and-developments/judge-stops-usdas-radical-restructuring-for-now','https://eos.org/research-and-developments/judge-stops-usdas-radical-restructuring-for-now','Judge Stops USDA’s Radical Restructuring, For Now','judgestopsusdasradicalrestructuringfornow','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-14T18:57:55Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','Credit: Adam Fagen, Flickr , CC BY-NC-SA 2.0',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(172,'https://eos.org/research-and-developments/2026-state-of-the-climate-report-shows-a-planet-reaching-its-limits','https://eos.org/research-and-developments/2026-state-of-the-climate-report-shows-a-planet-reaching-its-limits','2026 State of the Climate Report Shows a Planet Reaching Its Limits','2026stateoftheclimatereportshowsaplanetreachingitslimits','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-14T13:00:48Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','The Upsala Glacier in Argentina, like many of Earth''s glaciers, has been thinning and retreating at a rapid pace. According to a new report, worldwide glacier thickness is at a record low. Credit: Etienne Berthier, Université de Toulouse/NASA Goddard',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(173,'https://eos.org/opinions/what-we-could-lose-trying-to-make-mars-habitable','https://eos.org/opinions/what-we-could-lose-trying-to-make-mars-habitable','What We Could Lose by Trying to Make Mars Habitable','whatwecouldlosebytryingtomakemarshabitable','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-14T12:46:11Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','Layers of ice on Mars, like those viewed in Chasma Boreale by the High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment (HiRISE), hold a fragile record of climate fluctuations. Credit: NASA/JPL/Univ. of Arizona',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(174,'https://eos.org/research-spotlights/patchier-permafrost-is-linked-to-more-intense-hillslope-erosion','https://eos.org/research-spotlights/patchier-permafrost-is-linked-to-more-intense-hillslope-erosion','Patchier Permafrost Is Linked to More Intense Hillslope Erosion','patchierpermafrostislinkedtomoreintensehillslopeerosion','eos','Eos (AGU)','en','2026-10-14T12:43:30Z','2026-10-17T08:44:22Z','Researchers collect a sediment core from a lake in Alaska’s Toolik Lake cluster to investigate erosion across permafrost landscapes. Credit: Mark Abbott',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(175,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wider-approach-mitigate-rechargeable-battery.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wider-approach-mitigate-rechargeable-battery.html','Wider approach needed to mitigate rising rechargeable battery emissions','widerapproachneededtomitigaterisingrechargeablebatteryemissions','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T20:10:02Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','A new University of Melbourne-led study has identified insights and evidence that could help mitigate the fast-rising climate impact of lithium-ion batteries (LIBs).',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(176,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-natural-gas-midwest-city-leaks.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-natural-gas-midwest-city-leaks.html','About 2% of natural gas supplied to a Midwest city leaks into the atmosphere','about2ofnaturalgassuppliedtoamidwestcityleaksintotheatmosphere','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T19:30:02Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Most natural gas leakage in Indianapolis likely originates from homes and other buildings, not exterior pipelines and large-scale distribution infrastructure that deliver the fossil fuel, according to Penn State researchers who assessed nearly a decade of data.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(177,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-supercooled-clouds-highest-mountains-differently.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-supercooled-clouds-highest-mountains-differently.html','Supercooled clouds over the highest mountains respond differently to climate change','supercooledcloudsoverthehighestmountainsresponddifferentlytoclimatechange','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T18:10:01Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Most studies of how clouds are responding to climate change focus on clouds over the oceans, rather than over mountains. Those that do focus on mountain clouds tend to explore cloud microphysics rather than long-term trends in cloud phase. But understanding cloud behavior over regions such as the Tibetan Plateau, which supplies water to hundreds of millions of people, is critical—especially as diminishing snowpack and altered precipitation patterns threaten this critical water source.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(178,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-climate-simulation.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-climate-simulation.html','Forecasting ''tipping points'' with AI climate simulation','forecastingtippingpointswithaiclimatesimulation','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T17:40:01Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','The writer Malcolm Gladwell popularized the term "tipping points" to refer to the moment when a trend or social behavior crosses a threshold and rapidly spreads everywhere. In physics, tipping points occur when systems—say, Earth''s climate—reach an unstable point and topple over into a sea of change that is sometimes irreversible. Examples include global warming or the melting of polar ice caps.',3.0,'classified',NULL,11);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(179,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wetland-reclamation-limited-agricultural-gains.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wetland-reclamation-limited-agricultural-gains.html','Wetland reclamation brings limited agricultural gains but high flood exposure','wetlandreclamationbringslimitedagriculturalgainsbuthighfloodexposure','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T17:20:01Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Wetland reclamation has long been regarded as a strategy to meet growing food demand, given the favorable soil and water conditions of wetlands. However, the trade-off between potentially greater crop production and the ecological cost of lost wetlands—especially involving flood regulation—has not been well understood.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(180,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-excess-oxygen-deep-earth-magma.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-excess-oxygen-deep-earth-magma.html','''Excess oxygen'' stored in deep Earth magma may help explain how Earth''s atmosphere became oxygen-rich','excessoxygenstoredindeepearthmagmamayhelpexplainhowearthsatmospherebecameoxygenrich','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T17:00:14Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','A research team has found that molten rock can carry more oxygen than its chemical composition would seem to allow. Their study is published in National Science Review.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(181,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nasa-satellites-historic-el-nio.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nasa-satellites-historic-el-nio.html','NASA satellites observe historic 2026 El Niño','nasasatellitesobservehistoric2026elnino','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T16:44:08Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','A phenomenon rarer than a solar eclipse and more powerful than any single storm system is unfolding this fall, and NASA is tracking it closely.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(182,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wastewater-treatment-local-demand-china.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wastewater-treatment-local-demand-china.html','Matching wastewater treatment to local demand could help China tackle water scarcity','matchingwastewatertreatmenttolocaldemandcouldhelpchinatacklewaterscarcity','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T16:20:06Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Wastewater reclamation is increasingly regarded as an alternative water source to mitigate water scarcity in China. However, expanding wastewater treatment capacity alone may not be enough to ease this scarcity, since wastewater supply, treatment facilities and reuse demand are not always geographically aligned.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(183,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wood-global-agenda-outline-core.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-wood-global-agenda-outline-core.html','Putting wood security on the global agenda: Researchers outline 20 core challenges','puttingwoodsecurityontheglobalagendaresearchersoutline20corechallenges','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T15:40:03Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','A study led by the Centre for Global Wood Security formally defines the concept of global wood security and identifies the 20 greatest challenges to wood security in the 21st century.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(184,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-plastic-packaging-purpose-wont-recycling.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-plastic-packaging-purpose-wont-recycling.html','Could your plastic film packaging serve a purpose? We won''t know until recycling laws change','couldyourplasticfilmpackagingserveapurposewewontknowuntilrecyclinglawschange','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T15:00:08Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','From the clear covering on your microwave meal to the flimsy bread bag lying on the kitchen counter, plastic film is notoriously tricky to recycle. But what if those annoying bits of plastic waste could be put to better use?',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(185,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-spain-sizzles-hottest-september-weather.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-spain-sizzles-hottest-september-weather.html','Spain sizzles in hottest September on record: Weather agency','spainsizzlesinhottestseptemberonrecordweatheragency','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T14:45:35Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Mainland Spain sizzled in its warmest September since records began in 1961, with exceptional heat effectively making it another summer month, national weather agency AEMET said on Friday.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(186,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-combined-sewer-affect-quality-philadelphia.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-combined-sewer-affect-quality-philadelphia.html','How do combined sewer overflows affect water quality in Philadelphia?','howdocombinedseweroverflowsaffectwaterqualityinphiladelphia','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T14:00:09Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','In Philadelphia and many urban areas across the U.S. with sewer systems dating back to the 1800s, more frequent flooding is posing a threat not only to people and property but also to surrounding ecosystems. In these areas, stormwater and sewage drain into the same pipes, which are designed to spill into nearby waterways when flooding occurs rather than inundating streets.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(187,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-wastewater-dna-based-sewer.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-ai-wastewater-dna-based-sewer.html','AI and wastewater DNA-based mapping help identify sewer pipes at risk of corrosion','aiandwastewaterdnabasedmappinghelpidentifysewerpipesatriskofcorrosion','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T13:38:48Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Underground sewers that safely manage wastewater are essential to modern cities. However, since they are buried underground and difficult to access, their condition can be hard to monitor. Traditional camera inspections are costly and time-consuming, but we need regular inspections so aging pipes do not continue to deteriorate until they reach a breaking point. When damaged pipes leak, they can pose serious risks to public health by contaminating surrounding water, while more severe failures can even damage infrastructure above them, causing sinkholes in city streets.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(188,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-molecular-fingerprints-reveal-human-worldwide.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-molecular-fingerprints-reveal-human-worldwide.html','Molecular fingerprints reveal how human activities alter water systems worldwide','molecularfingerprintsrevealhowhumanactivitiesalterwatersystemsworldwide','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T13:00:06Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','A Florida State University–led review shows how ultrahigh-resolution mass spectrometry can help researchers identify the molecular fingerprints of human activity in water systems around the world.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(189,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-winter-sea-ice-loss-underestimated.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-winter-sea-ice-loss-underestimated.html','Winter sea ice loss may be underestimated, ancient clues from a warmer Arctic suggest','winterseaicelossmaybeunderestimatedancientcluesfromawarmerarcticsuggest','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T09:00:04Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Future Arctic winter sea ice is likely to shrink by around 1 million square kilometers (386,000 square miles) more than previously thought, after researchers used clues from a warm period 6,000 years ago to improve the reliability of climate models.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(190,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-depth-chemicals-finger-lakes.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-depth-chemicals-finger-lakes.html','First in-depth map of ''forever chemicals'' in the Finger Lakes created','firstindepthmapofforeverchemicalsinthefingerlakescreated','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-16T03:40:01Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','In 2023, professor Damian Helbling was getting a lot of calls and emails from local residents. The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) had just announced regulations to limit per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS)—some of which have been linked to cancers and other negative health effects—in drinking water. Ithacans and others in the region wanted to know about levels in the ponds, lakes, streams and creeks in their proverbial backyards.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(191,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-white-sands-footprints-humans-experienced.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-white-sands-footprints-humans-experienced.html','White Sands footprints suggest humans experienced abrupt environmental shifts during the last ice age','whitesandsfootprintssuggesthumansexperiencedabruptenvironmentalshiftsduringthelasticeage','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T23:20:08Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Expanded excavations at White Sands National Park in New Mexico have provided researchers an opportunity to refine the age of the oldest human footprints in North America and establish a deeper understanding of the landscape and environmental conditions at the time humans were present there.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(192,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-national-explore-broader-future.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-national-explore-broader-future.html','Beyond national parks: Researchers explore a broader future for conservation','beyondnationalparksresearchersexploreabroaderfutureforconservation','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T22:00:01Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','As governments prepare to gather in Armenia for the U.N. biodiversity summit, researchers are highlighting a conservation approach that recognizes places where nature thrives beyond conventional protected areas.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(193,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-deep-ocean-oxygen-microscopic-magnetic.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-deep-ocean-oxygen-microscopic-magnetic.html','Measuring deep-ocean oxygen with microscopic magnetic fossils','measuringdeepoceanoxygenwithmicroscopicmagneticfossils','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T21:40:06Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Humanity''s ongoing disruption of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO₂) concentrations, temperatures, and global nutrient cycles is starving the oceans of oxygen. Although we can measure ongoing changes to ocean circulation and marine life, we still don''t know how far the oxygen loss and the damage to ocean ecosystems could go. One way to foresee the oceans'' future is to look back at how our seas and their inhabitants responded to higher CO₂ levels and warmer climates in the past.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(194,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-north-sea-fueled-underwater-plankton.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-north-sea-fueled-underwater-plankton.html','North Sea heat wave fueled underwater plankton bloom largely missed by satellites','northseaheatwavefueledunderwaterplanktonbloomlargelymissedbysatellites','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T20:00:07Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','When a marine heat wave strikes, scientists often turn to satellites to see how the ocean is responding. From space, changes in ocean color can reveal shifts in phytoplankton, microscopic organisms that form the foundation of marine food webs and play a crucial role in the global carbon cycle. But satellites have a fundamental limitation: they can only see the ocean''s surface.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(195,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-california-earthquakes-validate-day-seismic.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-california-earthquakes-validate-day-seismic.html','Nearly 600 California earthquakes help validate models that forecast next-day seismic risk','nearly600californiaearthquakeshelpvalidatemodelsthatforecastnextdayseismicrisk','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T18:40:01Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','The University of Bristol–led study, published in Nature Communications, could help millions of people living in earthquake-prone countries make better-informed decisions about seismic risk, particularly during ongoing periods of increased earthquake activity.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(196,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-global-ice-cliff-survey-disputed.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-global-ice-cliff-survey-disputed.html','Global ice-cliff survey tests disputed theory behind projections of accelerated sea level rise','globalicecliffsurveytestsdisputedtheorybehindprojectionsofacceleratedsealevelrise','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T18:00:20Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','A new study led by the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution (WHOI) provides the most comprehensive observational analysis yet of the towering ice cliffs that fringe Earth''s ice sheets, giving scientists new evidence to evaluate a long-debated process that could influence future sea level rise. Improving these projections is increasingly important as rising seas threaten coastal communities and infrastructure worldwide.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(197,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-urges-action-poisoning-dangers.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-urges-action-poisoning-dangers.html','WHO urges action on lead poisoning dangers','whourgesactiononleadpoisoningdangers','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T16:40:05Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','The World Health Organization on Thursday told countries to do more to prevent lead exposure, with hundreds of millions of children affected worldwide.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(198,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-scorecard-colombia-high-emitting-countries.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-scorecard-colombia-high-emitting-countries.html','Scorecard: Colombia gets top marks while high-emitting countries doing too little to protect health from climate change','scorecardcolombiagetstopmarkswhilehighemittingcountriesdoingtoolittletoprotecthealthfromclimatechange','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T15:00:15Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','The latest Healthy NDC Scorecard, published by the Global Climate and Health Alliance, finds that, Colombia integrates health most deeply into its national climate plan, closely followed by Nigeria and Cambodia. Conversely, the climate commitments of the governments of Japan, New Zealand and India fail to adequately consider health, while high-emitting economies, including EU members, China, Brazil and the U.K., refer to health in their national climate plans but do not match this with the ambitious emissions reduction targets required to protect health.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(199,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-qa-climate-capture-complexities-health.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-qa-climate-capture-complexities-health.html','Q&A: Expanding climate models to better capture the complexities of public health','qaexpandingclimatemodelstobettercapturethecomplexitiesofpublichealth','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T12:40:05Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','About a decade ago, a group of climate scientists described five scenarios for the future. Called the Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), these scenarios suggest how socioeconomic development could evolve in worlds with differing challenges to climate adaptation and mitigation.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(200,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-countries-vulnerable-fossil-fuel-phaseout.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-countries-vulnerable-fossil-fuel-phaseout.html','Countries most vulnerable to fossil fuel phaseout generate just 6% of global output, climate analysis finds','countriesmostvulnerabletofossilfuelphaseoutgeneratejust6ofglobaloutputclimateanalysisfinds','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T09:00:12Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Countries highly vulnerable to the phase-out of oil, gas and coal account for just 6% of global economic output, countries in Africa stand to benefit from their vast solar energy potential, and the economic interests of the U.S., Europe and China are affected by climate policy in surprisingly similar ways. A study by the Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK) published in Nature Sustainability now shows, for 169 countries around the world, how the fight against global heating affects national assets—and what geopolitical challenges and opportunities arise as a result.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(201,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-delivery-gap-national-action.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-climate-delivery-gap-national-action.html','Climate adaptation''s delivery gap: Turning national plans into action','climateadaptationsdeliverygapturningnationalplansintoaction','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T02:00:11Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','By 2025, 143 of 154 developing countries had a National Adaptation Plan process in place, yet implementation has not kept pace. Many countries still lack the institutional capacity, project preparation and finance needed to turn their priorities into strategies, bankable projects and results on the ground.',1.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(202,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nepal-triggered-ice-avalanche.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nepal-triggered-ice-avalanche.html','Nepal flood triggered by rock and ice avalanche: Report','nepalfloodtriggeredbyrockandiceavalanchereport','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-15T01:00:01Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Nepal''s disaster authority said Wednesday that an avalanche of rock and ice triggered a recent flash flood, echoing the mountain collapse that unleashed a tsunami-like wall of water in August.',2.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(203,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-fine-particles-aircraft-exhaust-lab.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-fine-particles-aircraft-exhaust-lab.html','Fine particles from aircraft exhaust become more harmful over time, lab tests suggest','fineparticlesfromaircraftexhaustbecomemoreharmfulovertimelabtestssuggest','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-14T23:20:02Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','Fine particulate matter consists of tiny particles with diameters in the nanometer range. They are produced, for example, by fuel combustion, remain suspended in the air and can penetrate deep into the respiratory tract. There, they can trigger oxidative stress, a process that promotes inflammation and, over the long term, can lead to serious health problems such as respiratory or cardiovascular diseases. Scientists call this harmful property the particles'' oxidative potential.',0.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO "items" VALUES(204,'https://phys.org/news/2026-10-warmer-climate-reshape-hurricane-relationship.html','https://phys.org/news/2026-10-warmer-climate-reshape-hurricane-relationship.html','Warmer climate could reshape hurricane risk and its relationship with El Niño around Hawai''i','warmerclimatecouldreshapehurricaneriskanditsrelationshipwithelninoaroundhawaii','phys_earth','Phys.org Earth news','en','2026-10-14T21:20:17Z','2026-10-17T08:44:23Z','A new study examining how tropical cyclone activity near Hawai''i could change in a warmer future finds a shift toward stronger storms, even though substantial uncertainty remains about whether the total number of storms near the islands will increase.',3.0,'filtered_out',NULL,NULL);
